int to a comfortable win; the main reason to stay at a high-single-digit margin rather than the Safe D consensus is thin polling showing leads of one to seventeen points.", "watch": "If late surveys show Hull closing into the low single digits, or the debate-skipping standoff becomes a bigger story, the race could tighten toward Lean D territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Michelle Lujan Grisham (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's open governor's race pits Democrat Deb Haaland against Republican Gregg Hull, with term-limited Michelle Lujan Grisham leaving the seat vacant. Haaland enters with clear advantages: the state leans Democratic by about four points, and she has raised roughly $11 million to Hull's half-million. Ratings from seven analysts range from Likely D to Safe D, and prediction markets put her chances in the mid-90s.\n\nPolling is sparse but consistently favors Haaland — a late-August survey of likely voters showed a six-point lead, and even a Republican-leaning pollster had her ahead. Hull has struggled to break past the low-to-mid 40s, a familiar ceiling for recent New Mexico Republican statewide runs. The main wrinkles are Haaland's decision to skip debates and a thin polling record, but the overall evidence points to a comfortable Democratic hold of the open seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9664, "margin": 10.58}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9605, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.948, "margin": 10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9415, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9502, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9552, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9463, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9506, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9559, "margin": 9.5}]}